192 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 54 0 R/E(JPFA now earns at a structurally higher level than it did in the previous cycle. Profit to owners ranged from Rp 0.9tn to Rp 2.0tn in FY20-FY23. Since FY24 it has been Rp 3.0-4.0tn, and 1H26 alone earned Rp 2.5tn. Demand supports the new level: national feed output is forecast to reach 23.6m tonnes by 2026/27 \(USDA FAS, 2026a\). Yet at 5.0x trailing earnings, the share price implies an 11.6% WACC against our 10.46%, as if profit were returning to the old range. We forecast profit to owners of Rp 4.4-5.3tn a year in FY26F-FY30F, so we see the stock as mispriced.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

193 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 54 0 R/E(Part of the step up came from cheaper feed inputs, which lifted the gross margin before D&A from 16.3% in FY23 to 23.2% in FY25, and we do not assume that lasts. In our model segment margins revert halfway to their FY21\20525 averages each year. Profit still holds at Rp 4.4\2055.0tn a year after FY26F, because revenue keeps growing 9.7% a year, processing adds a steadier stream of profit and debt is far lower, with net debt down from 3.5x EBITDA in FY23 to 1.1x in FY25. Even our lowest year, FY28F, is more than twice the old-cycle peak \(Figure 1.6\). At Rp 2,250 the shares trade at 5.0x FY26F earnings and 5.3\2055.9x after that, and a 40% payout gives a dividend yield of 6.7\2058.0%, which pays holders while the gap to fair value closes.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

