238 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 54 0 R/E(JPFA\220s integration lets it price feed cost-plus and keeps its balance sheet steady through the cycle. Integration also creates structural volume: a large share of each segment\220s sales goes to other JPFA segments, averaging 56.2% of animal feed sales, 61.3% of poultry breeding sales, 14.6% of commercial farm sales and 3.9% of poultry processing sales over 2020\20525 \(Figure 2.2\). With more than half of feed and breeding sales going to its own farms, those segments keep a captive buyer even when outside demand is weak.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[53]>>
endobj

239 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 54 0 R/E(JPFA is Indonesia\220s second-largest producer of feed and DOC. It runs 15 feed mills, 79 breeding farms and 30 hatcheries, supported by more- )/Pg 12 0 R/K[54]>>
endobj

240 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 54 0 R/E(JPFA is one of Indonesia\220s largest integrated poultry-food companies, with about 22% of national feed capacity and 25% of day-old chick \(DOC\) capacity \(JPFA, 2026c\). Its economic moat is a high degree of integration from upstream to downstream across six business segments \(Figure 2.4\). Upstream, it makes animal feed and breeds its own birds, from imported grandparent stock \(GPS\) down to livebirds. Downstream, it sells ready-to-cook and processed foods under nationally known brands \(Figure 2.1\). In between, the midstream runs hundreds of commercial farms and processing plants, and aquaculture and trading sit alongside the poultry chain. Poultry makes up about 88% of revenue.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[55]>>
endobj

241 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 54 0 R/E(This vertical integration gives JPFA control over its supply chain and a steady base of internal volume.)/Pg 12 0 R/K[56]>>
endobj

