297 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 56 0 R/E(We size MBG\220s chicken needs from the programme\220s technical guidelines, using 71m average 2026 recipients, weighted service days, chicken on 40% of days \(our assumption\) and 78g per serving. That gives about 549kt, roughly 14% of national demand of 3.9m tonnes, with a range of 381\205867kt across our bear and bull cases \(Figure 3.7, Annex 13\). USDA puts MBG chicken demand at 1.0\2051.1m tonnes, a figure that also appears as a government production target, so we treat it as a policy reference. MBG also favours larger birds, with grow-out lengthening from about 30 days to 35\20540 days \(USDA FAS, 2026a\), so we expect it to help JPFA mainly through feed volumes.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

298 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 56 0 R/E(Demand: MBG Could Add About 14%)/Pg 14 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

299 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 56 0 R/E(CPIN and MAIN run integrated models like JPFA\220s, from feed and breeding to farms and processed food, yet their share prices have diverged. Over three years JPFA returned +105%, against \21231% for CPIN, +67% for MAIN and \2126% for the JCI \(Yahoo Finance, 2026\). The 2026 GPS allocation is the main competitive challenge. JPFA\220s share fell from 28.2% to 20.6%, while Berdikari took 11.9% \(Figure 3.3\). If JPFA\220s DOC capacity stays at its allocated 165,000 birds while national supply grows, its share of final-stock DOC output could fall from about 30% to 21% by 2028 \(Annex 10\). Integration still gives JPFA an internal outlet for most of its breeding output, but competition for outside DOC sales will get tougher.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

