endobj

470 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 58 0 R/E(Raw Materials Set the Margin, Feed Holds It)/Pg 16 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

471 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 58 0 R/E(Cost of goods takes 77\20584% of revenue, and raw materials make up 90% of it, so the price of corn and soybean meal sets JPFA\220s margin. From FY23 to FY25 revenue grew 8.9% a year while cost of goods grew only 4.3% as feed inputs eased, so gross profit grew 29.9% a year and the gross margin before D&A rose from 16.3% to 23.2% \(Figure 5.1\). We do not assume that spread lasts. Segment margins revert halfway to their FY21\20525 averages each year, so the gross margin eases to 20.3% by FY30F and the EBITDA margin from 12.1% in FY26F to 8.6%. Most of that decline comes from breeding and commercial farming, which sell at the daily livebird price. Commercial farming\220s segment result falls from Rp 1.8tn in FY25 to Rp 0.5\2050.7tn in FY27F\205FY28F as its margin returns toward a thin FY21\20525 average, yet the group still earns Rp 4.4\2054.5tn for owners at an EBITDA margin of 9.4\20510.0%. Those are also the years when the 2026 GPS quota lands as extra chicks \(Figure 3.4\). We assume demand growth, MBG included, absorbs that supply, so our base case keeps livebird prices rising with inflation. If half of the supply gap reached prices, FY28F profit to owners would fall to Rp 2.7tn \(R1\). Feed carries the group. Its cost-plus margin stays in its band and its segment result rises to Rp 4.8tn, about 48% of the total \(Figure 5.2\). Even if the supply wave and a 25% rise in corn and SBM come in the same years, our model still gives Rp 2.0\2052.2tn of profit to owners, level with the old peak. This is Thesis 1 in the numbers.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

472 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 58 0 R/E(Processing Changes the Mix)/Pg 16 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

473 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 58 0 R/E(Poultry processing and consumer products \(PPCP\) is the fastest-growing part of the group. Its sales rose 29.8% year on year in 1H26, and its capex of Rp 459bn in 1H26 is already above the Rp 380bn spent in all of FY25. Group revenue rises from Rp 60.7tn in FY25 to Rp 108.3tn in FY30F, and processing supplies more than half of that increase, with sales growing from Rp 10.1tn, 16.7% of group sales, to Rp 37.9tn, 35.0%. Its segment result grows from Rp 0.44tn to Rp 1.62tn, passes commercial farming in FY27F and lifts its share of the group\220s segment result from 6% to 16% \(Figures 5.2 and 5.3\). PPCP earns a steadier 4\2055% margin and gains when livebird prices fall, because birds are its main input, so the shift makes group profit less exposed to the daily livebird price. )/Pg 16 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

