581 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 59 0 R/E(Returns, Cash Flow and Debt)/Pg 17 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

582 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 59 0 R/E(Return on equity rose from 6.7% in FY23 to 23.4% in FY25 and peaks at 25.4% in FY26F, then settles at 15\20519% as margins revert \(Figure 5.7\). Working capital is stable. The cash conversion cycle stays at 75\20582 days, set mostly by about 90 days of inventory, which is largely feed raw materials and growing birds. Operating cash flow was 1.2x net profit in FY25, and we expect 1.0\2051.1x in FY26F\205FY30F. Capex runs at more than twice depreciation while processing capacity is built, yet free cash flow stays positive at Rp 1.4\2052.4tn a year. The payout rose to 40% of FY25 profit, paid in 1H26, and from FY27F dividends run a little ahead of free cash flow, so net debt rises from Rp 7.2tn in FY26F to Rp 8.0\2058.5tn. The balance sheet can carry that. Net debt/EBITDA fell from 3.5x in FY23 to 1.1x in FY25 and stays at 1.0x or below through FY30F, interest cover rises from 2.2x to about 10x, and liabilities fall from 58% to 39% of total assets \(Figure 5.7; Annexes 1 and 2\). )/Pg 17 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

583 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 59 0 R/E(In March 2026 JPFA repaid its US$350m 5.375% notes with Rp 5tn of rupiah bank loans, and in July it issued Rp 500bn of rupiah bonds at 9.25\2059.50% \(Bisnis Indonesia, 2026; IDNFinancials, 2026\). Net foreign-currency liabilities fell from US$331m to US$68m \(Figure 5.9\), so each Rp 100/US$ fall in the rupiah now costs about Rp 7bn in translation loss, against Rp 33bn before. All but US$50m of the debt is in rupiah. Over FY26F\205FY30F free cash flow adds up to Rp 9.0tn against Rp 9.5tn of dividends paid, minorities included, so the payout is close to self-funding \(Figure 5.10\).)/Pg 17 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

